/-!
# Basic opens of an affine étale chart, and the supported sections over a chart

Let `F` be an algebraic space and let `U` be an étale chart of `F` whose underlying scheme
`X` is affine. For a global regular function `g` on `X` the Zariski open `X.basicOpen g`, read
as an étale chart of `F` by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.openChart`, is again an affine étale
chart; the regular functions on it are the localization of those on `X` away from `g`, with
restriction of functions as the structure map; and for a quasi-coherent `𝒪_F`-module the
sections over it are the corresponding localization of the sections over `U`.

That is what turns the ideal of part (1) of Stacks 0859 into the tag's description of the
sections supported on a closed subset: **part (2)**, that over an affine chart they are exactly
the sections annihilated by a power of that ideal.

**Part (3)** of the tag, that the supported sections form a quasi-coherent subsheaf, follows:
part (2) exhibits the supported sections over each affine chart as the ideal-power torsion of
the sections there, so the base change comparison of the subsheaf over a morphism of affine
charts is bijective as soon as ideal-power torsion commutes with the base change — which it
does, the restriction maps between affine étale charts being flat and the ideal finitely
generated.  Quasi-coherence is then that bijectivity read through Descent, Proposition 03DX (2).

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartScheme_openChart`: the scheme underlying a Zariski-open chart
  is the open subscheme itself, a definitional identity.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isAffine_chartScheme_openChart_basicOpen`: a basic open of an affine
  étale chart is an affine étale chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRestrict_openChartInclusion`: restriction of regular functions
  along the inclusion of a Zariski-open chart is the map of global functions induced by the
  inclusion of the open subscheme.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocalization_away_chartRing_openChart_basicOpen`: the regular
  functions on the basic-open chart of `g` are the localization away from `g`, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isUnit_chartRestrict_openChartInclusion_basicOpen` that `g` becomes
  invertible there.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocalizedModule_chartRestrictLinear_openChart_basicOpen`: for a
  module cartesian on affine charts the sections over the basic-open chart are the localization
  away from `g` of the sections over the chart, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRestrictLinear_openChart_basicOpen_eq_zero_iff` reads that as a
  vanishing criterion.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionSupportedIn_iff_exists_pow_le_annihilator` is **part (2) of
  Stacks 0859**.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.preimage_zeroLocus_ideal_map`: the preimage of the zero locus of an
  ideal of global functions is the zero locus of the ideal generated by its image.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_range_lTensor_of_pow_smul_eq_zero`: **ideal-power torsion for a
  finitely generated ideal commutes with flat base change.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartPointsMap_comp`: the map on points of a chart factors through
  the map on points of any chart over which it sits, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.preimage_chartPointsMap_eq_zeroLocus_map`: the ideal of part (1) for
  a chart cuts the subset out on every chart over it too, after pushing it forward along
  restriction of functions.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.bijective_sectionsBaseChange_supportedSectionsSubmodule`: the base
  change comparison of the supported-section subsheaf is bijective over a morphism of affine
  étale charts, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_supportedSectionsSubmodule` is **part (3) of
  Stacks 0859**.

## Implementation notes

`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRestrict` of a chart morphism is *definitionally* the induced map
of global functions of the underlying morphism of schemes
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRestrict_eq_appTop`): the structure presheaf of the small étale
site is `(SmallEtale.toOver ⋙ BigFppfOver.toScheme).op ⋙ Scheme.Γ`. What is not definitional is
that the underlying morphism of schemes of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.openChartInclusion` is the
inclusion of the open — that is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.toScheme_map_toOver_map_openChartInclusion`, which goes through
`B.inclusion.map_preimage` — so the identification of `chartRestrict` is that theorem
transported through `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.appTop`.

The localization statement is then Mathlib's
`AlgebraicGeometry.Γ_restrict_isLocalization`, whose algebra structure
`AlgebraicGeometry.ΓRestrictAlgebra` is `Scheme.Opens.ι.appTop` turned into an algebra; since
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartAlgebra` is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRestrict` turned into
an algebra, the two agree by the identification above, and the transport is a single
`congrArg RingHom.toAlgebra`. On the module side,
`isLocalizedModule_iff_isBaseChange` and `TensorProduct.isBaseChange` make the base change of
the sections a localization, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionsBaseChange` is bijective on
affine charts for a cartesian module, and restriction of sections is that comparison applied to
`1 ⊗ -`, so `IsLocalizedModule.comp_iff_of_bijective_left` transfers the property.

Part (2) is then the argument of the scheme-level
`AlgebraicGeometry.mem_supportedSectionsSubmodule_iff_exists_pow_le_annihilator` of Stacks
07ZP, read through the support API of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/SheafSupport.lean`. Two things make it short.
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroImages`, whose complement is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.supportOfSection`, is a *union* over all charts on which the section
restricts to zero, so the direction from torsion to supportedness only has to exhibit the
basic-open charts of the members of the ideal as covering the complement of the subset in the
chart — it says nothing about every chart over it. And the vanishing criterion above is an
*iff*, so both directions of part (2) come out of `IsLocalizedModule.eq_zero_iff` with no
separate unit-cancellation argument; the finite generation of the ideal is used exactly once,
in `Ideal.exists_pow_le_of_le_radical_of_fg`, to turn "each member kills the section after some
power" into one power of the ideal.

Quasi-coherence is a hypothesis of part (2) in the form Stacks 0859 states it, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCartesianOnAffineCharts` is *derived* from it by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCartesianOnAffineCharts_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`, Stacks 03DX,
rather than assumed separately.  The same holds of part (3), where the derivation is used in
both directions: quasi-coherence of `G` gives bijectivity of its own base change comparison,
and bijectivity of the comparison of the subsheaf gives back quasi-coherence of the subsheaf,
by the full equivalence
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_iff_isCartesianOnAffineCharts`.

Part (3) invokes part (1) only on the *larger* of the two affine charts.  The ideal cutting out
the subset on the smaller chart is taken to be the image of that one under restriction, which is
legitimate by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.preimage_chartPointsMap_eq_zeroLocus_map` and stays
finitely generated by `Ideal.FG.map`; `Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap` then says that a section over
the smaller chart is annihilated by a power of the image ideal exactly when it is annihilated,
through restriction, by that power of the ideal upstairs.  So both charts' supported sections
are described by powers of one ideal `I` of functions on the larger chart, and the comparison
becomes the purely algebraic statement that `I`-power torsion commutes with flat base change.
Taking two unrelated ideals from part (1) instead would require comparing their zero loci, which
is strictly more work for the same conclusion.

The algebraic statement itself,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_range_lTensor_of_pow_smul_eq_zero`, is not in Mathlib and is proved
here from `Module.Flat.lTensor_exact`: for `I ^ n` generated by a finite family `a_i`, the
submodule it annihilates is the kernel of `x ↦ (a_i x)_i`, flatness carries the exact sequence
`0 → ker → M → M ^ r` through the base change, and `TensorProduct.piRight` identifies the kernel
of the base changed map with the elements annihilated by every `a_i`, hence by `I ^ n`.  Finite
generation of the ideal is used exactly there, to keep the index set finite — which is also
where the hypothesis of part (1) that the complement of the subset is quasi-compact finally
earns its place.  Injectivity of the comparison of the subsheaf needs no part of this: it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_sectionsBaseChange_of_injective_app` applied to the inclusion
into `G`, flatness being the only input.

